fix(runtime/session): bound closed-Session retention in RuntimeSessionCoordinator

Problem (runbook #8, appendix A F4/F5): close_session() only flips the record to
CLOSED; RuntimeSessionCoordinator._sessions and ._generations are never popped,
so every Session id ever registered stays in memory for the process lifetime
(~1.1 MB / 1000 sessions of steady-state growth in scripts_diag s2). The
execution registry (terminal_capacity=512, ttl=900s) and the work scheduler
(lane popped on close) were already bounded; only the coordinator leaked.

Fix: when close_session() reaches CLOSED (no timed-out executions left), the
record is appended to a bounded recently-closed LRU (`closed_session_capacity`,
default 512, mirroring the registry's terminal history). Beyond the cap the
oldest CLOSED record is evicted from _sessions together with its _generations
entry; a record that somehow still owns live executions is never dropped.
Re-registering an id removes it from the LRU.

Why not pop on close immediately: callers and tests read snapshot_session()
after close (e.g. test_close_session_cancels_goal_stream expects the CLOSED
snapshot with the CANCELLED execution history), so recent CLOSED records stay
inspectable.

_generations semantics: the per-id generation is the fence that rejects stale
close_session(generation=old), stale scheduler lanes and stale registry handles
(registry history still keyed by (session_id, generation)) after a re-register.
Dropping it naively would restart an evicted id at generation 1 and let stale
work match the new generation. Instead the coordinator keeps a single
monotonic `_generation_floor` = max generation ever evicted, and a brand-new or
evicted id starts at floor + 1. So every generation handed out for an id is
strictly greater than any it had before (O(1) memory, no tombstone map).
Ids that never had evictions in between keep exact per-id numbering
(1, 2, ...), so existing tests are unchanged. Visible behaviour change only
for ids evicted from history: snapshot_session() returns None (callers already
treat None like CLOSED) and close_session(generation=old) returns existed=False
(stop_session_for_archive already treats that as "already closed/replaced").

